Find the dma-ranges of PCI devices behind bridges without a device tree node.

U-Boot binds PCI bridges without an ofnode and then takes a device's DMA offset
from its parent's node, so anything behind a bridge gets no offset. The Pi 5's
external PCIe port maps system memory at PCI address 0x10_0000_0000, so NVMe
commands never complete and the drive fails to probe with -110 (ETIMEDOUT).

--- a/drivers/core/device.c
+++ b/drivers/core/device.c
@@ -459,7 +459,14 @@
 	u64 size = 0;
 	int ret;

-	if (!CONFIG_IS_ENABLED(DM_DMA) || !parent || !dev_has_ofnode(parent))
+	/*
+	 * Discovered devices (e.g. PCI bridges) have no node of their own, but
+	 * their DMA still goes through the dma-ranges of the bus above them.
+	 */
+	while (parent && !dev_has_ofnode(parent))
+		parent = parent->parent;
+
+	if (!CONFIG_IS_ENABLED(DM_DMA) || !parent)
 		return 0;

 	/*
